WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.140
So this is manganese metal. You know, very
often in chemistry we use manganese in the

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.690
form of permanganate as an oxidizing agent
but this is manganese metal.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:17.359
Manganese is right in the middle of the row
of transition metals and it has 5 electrons,

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:21.179
which can give it a really very rich chemistry.

00:00:21.179 --> 00:00:24.880
So here you can see it is a brand new sample
although the bottle looks very, very old,

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.109
it is 100grams. So I am going to pour a small
amount of manganese out, so that we can have

00:00:30.109 --> 00:00:31.559
a closer look.

00:00:31.559 --> 00:00:41.710
One of the main uses of manganese at the moment
is as a catalyst for making the plastic which

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:48.480
you find in plastic water bottles. You can
see my big display of water bottles up there.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:56.730
And manganese is a key catalyst in the reaction
between xylene, which is the raw material

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.570
which you get from crude oil to make terephthalic
acid which is one of the components in this

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:08.799
plastic. And the reason it is important is
that manganese can add and lose electrons

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.460
which help in the reaction between xylene
and oxygen.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:21.359
There is some paper on the top.

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:27.270
Manganese is also important in old batteries,
the older batteries that you used in torches,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:35.950
the sort that leaked, contained manganese
dioxide.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.030
So here we have the manganese and we are going
to pour a small amount out so that we can

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:47.450
have a look at this element. And you can see
how the colour refracts off the top, really

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:54.529
a quite lovely sample of the manganese metal
itself. So we are looking at manganese crystals

00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:58.889
growing from the surface because this material
would have been made from a high temperature,

00:01:58.889 --> 00:02:04.579
crystal growing-type process. So you can see
the manganese is growing and seeding new crystallites

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:08.420
of the surface - seeding off in every direction.
It’s cool.

